    "order": "given family",
    "conventions": "Most Ugandans carry an English, Christian or Muslim first name together with a name in their own language, and the local name is their own rather than a shared family surname: a father and his children often have different second names. People are often written surname first, like Nakato Sarah. Baganda names come from the father's clan, and women's forms usually start with Na-, like Nalubega beside the male Lubega. Twins have set names in Luganda: Waswa and Kato for boys, Babirye and Nakato for girls. Banyankole and Bakiga names are often short sentences about God or the family, like Tumusiime (let us thank him) or Ainembabazi (he has mercy), with girls' names often starting Ke-, Kyo- or Ko-. Acholi names mark the circumstances of birth: Okello for a boy born after twins, Akello for a girl, Opio and Apio for first-born twins.",
